work internally?**\n","\n","Suppose the input is 1,000 characters and your chunk size is 300 characters. The algorithm first attempts to split on `\\n\\n`.\n","\n","- If the resulting sections fit within 300 characters, it keeps them.\n","- If one section is 700 characters:\n","    - It attempts to split the 700-character section using `\\n`.\n","        - If that does not work, it tries `. `.\n","            - Then it tries spaces.\n","                - Finally, it may split at character boundaries.\n","\n","**Advantages**\n","\n","- Preserves meaningful structure when possible.\n","- Handles varying text sizes.\n","- Supports overlap.\n","- Easy to integrate with LangChain.\n","- Strong general-purpose baseline.\n","\n","**Disadvantages**\n","\n","- Character count is not the same as token count.\n","- Separator choices may not match document semantics.\n","- A section can still be split awkwardly.\n","\n","For many applications, recursive splitting is a good starting point.\n","\n","## Token-Based Chunking\n","\n","Token-based chunking splits documents according to the number of tokens rather than characters.\n","\n","Why is this useful? Because embedding models and LLMs have token-based input limits.\n","\n","The number of characters is not necessarily equal to the number of tokens. Tokenization depends on the model's tokenizer. For example, words like `ApproximateNearestNeighbor` may be split into multiple tokens.\n","\n","Token-based chunking provides better control over input lengths."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":10,"id":"a1eecc42","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-23T06:09:24.906962Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-23T06:09:24.906256Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-23T06:09:29.383753Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-23T06:09:29.382541Z"},"papermill":{"duration":4.490124,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:09:29.386015+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:09:24.895891+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"na