,"exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.03537+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["top_k = 3\n","\n","ranked_indices = np.argsort(reranking_scores)[::-1][:top_k]"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":29,"id":"807ba119","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.078193Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.077848Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.082876Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.08194Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.019067,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.084997+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.06593+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["top_documents = [\n","    candidate_documents[i]\n","    for i in ranked_indices[:top_k]\n","]"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":30,"id":"f2a413bb","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.108752Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.108332Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.115271Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.113939Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.021217,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.117427+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.09621+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Document: Machine learning is a branch of artificial intelligence.\n","Reranking Score: 10.950966\n","--------------------------------------------------\n","Document: Machine learning enables computers to learn from data.\n","Reranking Score: 8.937628\n","--------------------------------------------------\n","Document: Deep learning uses neural networks with multiple layers.\n","Reranking Score: -5.055693\n","--------------------------------------------------\n"]}],"source":["# Display reranked documents\n","for idx in ranked_indices[:top_k]:\n","    print(\"Document:\", candidate_documents[idx])\n","    print(\"Reranking Score:\", reranking_scores[idx])\n","    print(\"-\" * 50)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"0d194b38","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.010657,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.139295+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.128638+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Unlike a bi-encoder, the cross-encoder can use interactions between query and document tokens throughout its Transformer layers.\n","\n","For example, the model can evaluate whether:\n","\n","- The document answers the exact question.\n","- A specific concept is mentioned in the correct context.\n","- The document contains a contradiction or negation.\n","- The query's conditions are satisfied.\n","\n","### Why is a Cross-Encoder more accurate?\n","\n","Suppose:\n","\n","Query:\n","\n","```\n","Can Python execute multiple CPU-bound threads in parallel?\n","```\n","\n","Document A:\n","\n","```\n","Python supports multithreading, but the GIL in standard CPython\n","typically prevents multiple threads from executing Python bytecode\n","in parallel.\n","```\n","\n","Document B:\n","\n","```\n","Python supports multithreading, which allows multiple threads\n","to execute concurrently.\n","```\n","\n","- A bi-encoder might give both documents relatively high similarity.\n","- A cross-encoder can evaluate the detailed relationship between the question and answer and may rank Document A higher.\n","\n","The cross-encoder has access to both query and document simultaneously, which allows finer-grained relevance evaluation.\n","\n","### Why Don't We Use Cross-Encoders Directly for Vector Search?\n","\n","The answer is: computational cost.\n","\n","Imagine your vector database contains 1 million documents. You need to rank all of them against one user query.\n","\n","With a cross-encoder, you would need to process:\n","\n","$$ 1,000,000 \\text{ query-document pairs} $$\n","\n","That is expensive and slow.\n","\n","A bi-encoder allows us to precompute all document embeddings:\n","\n","$$ D_1 \\rightarrow V_1 $$\n","\n","$$ D_2 \\rightarrow V_2 $$\n","\n","$$ \\vdots $$\n","\n","$$ D_{1,000,000} \\rightarrow V_{1,000,000} $$\n","\n","Then an ANN index quickly searches the vectors.\n","\n","The practical architecture is:\n","\n","| Stage                 | Model             | Number of documents | Speed     |\n","| --------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------- | --------- |\n","| Initial retrieval     | Bi-Encoder + ANN  | 1 million           | Very fast |\n","| Reranking             | Cross-Encoder     | 50–200              | Slower    |\n","| LLM context selection | Final top results | 3–10                | Small     |\n","\n","The strategy is:\n","\n","Fast retrieval first, accurate ranking second.\n","\n","This is a two-stage retrieval architecture.\n","\n","## Types of Reranking Techniques\n","\n","Cross-encoder reranking is common, but it is not the only method.\n","\n","### Cross-Encoder Reranking\n","\n","Most common neural approach. A cross-encoder scores a query-document pair.\n","\n","Example models:\n","\n","- BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3\n","- cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2\n","- jina-reranker-v2-base-multilingual\n","\n","Typical usage:\n","\n","```\n","Query + Document 1 → Score 0.92\n","Query + Document 2 → Score 0.84\n","Query + Document 3 → Score 0.11\n","```\n","\n","Sort by score in descending order. Use it when relevance accuracy matters.\n","\n","### LLM-Based Reranking\n","\n","Instead of a dedicated reranker model, you can use a large language model.\n","\n","Example prompt:\n","\n","```\n","You are a document relevance evaluator.\n","\n","Query:\n","\"What is the difference between L1 and L2 regularization?\"\n","\n","Documents:\n","1. L1 regularization adds the absolute values of model weights\n","   to the loss function.\n","\n","2. L2 regularization adds the squared values of model weights\n","   to the loss function.\n","\n","3. Neural networks use activation functions such as ReLU.\n","\n","Rank the documents based on relevance to the query.\n","Return the ranking in JSON format.\n","```\n","\n","The LLM might return:\n","\n","```\n","{\n","  \"ranking\": [2, 1, 3]\n","}\n","```\n","\n","**Advantages**\n","\n","- Can understand complex semantic requirements.\n","- Can follow custom relevance instructions.\n","- Can explain ranking decisions.\n","- Useful for nuanced or domain-specific tasks.\n","\n","**Disadvantages**\n","\n","- Higher latency.\n","- Higher cost.\n","- Output may be inconsistent.\n","- May require structured output validation.\n","\n","For many production systems, a dedicated cross-encoder is more efficient than using an LLM for every reranking request.\n","\n","### Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)\n","\n","This is especially useful in hybrid search.\n","\n","Suppose you have two retrieval systems:\n","\n","- BM25 keyword search\n","- Vector semantic search\n","\n","BM25 returns:\n","\n","| Document | Rank |\n","| -------- | ---- |\n","| A        | 1    |\n","| B        | 2    |\n","| C        | 3    |\n","\n","Vector search returns:\n","\n","| Document | Rank |\n","| -------- | ---- |\n","| C        | 1    |\n","| A        | 2    |\n","| D        | 3    |\n","\n","We can combine the rankings using Reciprocal Rank Fusion.\n","\n","The formula is:\n","\n","$$ RRF(d)=\\sum_{r \\in R} \\frac{1}{k+\\operatorname{rank}_r(d)} $$\n","\n","Where:\n","\n","- $d$ = document\n","- $R$ = retrieval result lists\n","- $k$ = smoothing constant, commonly 60\n","- $\\operatorname{rank}_r(d)$ = document rank in a retrieval list\n","\n","Example:\n","\n","For Document A:\n","\n","$$ RRF(A)=\\frac{1}{60+1}+\\frac{1}{60+2} $$\n","\n","$$ RRF(A)\\approx0.03252 $$\n","\n","For Document C:\n","\n","$$ RRF(C)=\\frac{1}{60+3}+\\frac{1}{60+1} $$\n","\n","$$ RRF(C)\\approx0.03227 $$\n","\n","Document A ranks slightly higher than Document C.\n","\n","RRF combines rankings from multiple retrieval systems without requiring their raw scores to be on the same scale.\n","\n","**Important:** RRF is a rank-fusion technique, not a neural relevance model. It can be used before cross-encoder reranking."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":31,"id":"02ebe195","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.163563Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.162533Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.069828Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.068558Z"},"papermill":{"duration":2.922158,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.072124+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:47.149966+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"]}],"source":["%pip install -q langchain-classic langchain-community rank-bm25"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":32,"id":"0030aa66","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.096202Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.095796Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.42318Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.422061Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.34337,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.426269+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.082899+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["from langchain_classic.retrievers import EnsembleRetriever\n","from langchain_community.retrievers import BM25Retriever\n","\n","# Keyword-based retriever\n","bm25_retriever = BM25Retriever.from_documents(documents)\n","bm25_retriever.k = 5\n","\n","# Vector-based retriever\n","vector_retriever = vectorstore.as_retriever(\n","    search_kwargs={\"k\": 5}\n",")\n","\n","# Combine using Reciprocal Rank Fusion\n","ensemble_retriever = EnsembleRetriever(\n","    retrievers=[\n","        bm25_retriever,\n","        vector_retriever\n","    ],\n","    weights=[0.5, 0.5],\n","    c=60\n",")\n","\n","# Retrieve fused results\n","results = ensemble_retriever.invoke(\"What is machine learning?\")"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":33,"id":"ce70e7fc","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.453624Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.45304Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.460198Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.459296Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.021462,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.462153+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.440691+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"text/plain":["[Document(metadata={'source': 'doc1'}, page_content='Machine learning is a subset of artificial intelligence.'),\n"," Document(metadata={'source': 'doc3'}, page_content='RAG combines retrieval with generative AI.'),\n"," Document(metadata={'source': 'doc2'}, page_content='Deep learning uses neural networks with multiple layers.')]"]},"execution_count":33,"metadata":{},"output_type":"execute_result"}],"source":["results"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"9876774b","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.010931,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.483933+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.473002+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Optimal Retrieval Size\n","\n","Suppose the database contains 100,000 documents.\n","\n","You can choose:\n","\n","| Initial retrieval K | Final reranking K | Characteristics                                  |\n","| ------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n","| 10                  | 5                 | Low latency, may miss relevant documents         |\n","| 50                  | 5                 | Good balance                                     |\n","| 100                 | 5                 | Better candidate coverage, higher cost           |\n","| 500                 | 5                 | More expensive, diminishing returns              |\n","| 10,000              | 5                 | Usually impractical for a standard cross-encoder |\n","\n","The initial candidate size is commonly somewhere around 20–200, but the optimal value depends on your application, dataset, model, and latency requirements.\n","\n","Consider this example:\n","\n","- Your vector database contains 1 million documents.\n","- ANN retrieval returns 100 candidates.\n","- A reranker evaluates those 100 candidates.\n","- You select the best 5.\n","- The LLM receives those 5 documents.\n","\n","This is far more efficient than using a cross-encoder to score all 1 million documents.\n","\n","### The recall trade-off\n","\n","Let's say the most relevant document exists in the database. But the vector database only retrieves 20 candidates, and the correct document is ranked 21st. The reranker never sees that document.\n","\n","Therefore:\n","\n","$$ \\text{Reranking Quality} \\leq \\text{Candidate Retrieval Coverage} $$\n","\n","This inequality is a conceptual limit rather than a formal universal metric.\n","\n","It explains why increasing the candidate pool can improve the final result, even if the reranker itself does not change.\n","\n","## Reranking Scores vs Vector Similarity Scores\n","\n","Suppose your initial vector search produces:\n","\n","| Document | Cosine similarity |\n","| -------- | ----------------- |\n","| A        | 0.91              |\n","| B        | 0.88              |\n","| C        | 0.82              |\n","\n","The reranker produces:\n","\n","| Document | Reranker score |\n","| -------- | -------------- |\n","| A        | 2.1            |\n","| B        | 8.7            |\n","| C        | -1.2           |\n","\n","The final ranking is:\n","\n","1. B\n","2. A\n","3. C\n","\n","Why?\n","\n","Because the reranker is using a different model and a different scoring function.\n","\n","The score of 8.7 is not comparable to the cosine similarity of 0.88.\n","\n","### Important rules\n","\n","- Vector similarity score: Measures embedding-space similarity.\n","- Cross-encoder score: Measures model-estimated query-document relevance.\n","- BM25 score: Measures keyword relevance based on term matching.\n","- RRF score: Combines rankings from multiple result lists.\n","\n","Do not combine these raw values directly without a carefully designed normalization or fusion method.\n","\n","Also, a reranker score is not automatically a probability.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```\n","Reranker score = 8.7\n","```\n","\n","This does not mean:\n","\n","```\n","87% probability that the document is relevant\n","```\n","\n","## Reranking in Hybrid Search and RAG\n","\n","In many production RAG systems, vector search alone is not enough. We combine keyword search with semantic search.\n","\n","For example, consider this query:\n","\n","> \"What does error code ERR-1042 mean in our payment service?\"\n","\n","A semantic vector search might find documents about payment failures.\n","\n","But keyword search is very good at finding the exact error code `ERR-1042`.\n","\n","We can combine both.\n","\n","![RAG Reranking Pipeline](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/rag_reranking_pipeline.png)\n","\n","This approach combines the strengths of three systems:\n","\n","| System        | Strength                                |\n","| ------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n","| BM25          | Exact keywords, IDs, technical terms    |\n","| Vector search | Semantic meaning, conceptual similarity |\n","| Cross-encoder | Detailed query-document relevance       |\n","\n","For example, if you're building an AI assistant over technical documentation, this hybrid architecture can be very effective.\n","\n","## RRF vs Reranking\n","\n","A useful distinction:\n","\n","- RRF combines rankings from multiple retrieval systems.\n","- Cross-encoder reranking uses a neural model to score query-document relevance.\n","- Both can change document order.\n","- They solve different problems.\n","\n","A common sequence is:\n","\n","```\n","BM25 + Vector Search\n","        ↓\n","      RRF\n","        ↓\n","Candidate Documents\n","        ↓\n","Cross-Encoder Reranking\n","        ↓\n","Final Documents\n","        ↓\n","       LLM\n","```\n","\n","## Advanced Reranking Techniques\n","\n","### Metadata-aware reranking\n","\n","Suppose a user asks:\n","\n","> \"How do I deploy a FastAPI application using Docker?\"\n","\n","The documents have metadata:\n","\n","```json\n","{\n","    \"content\": \"Deploying FastAPI with Docker...\",\n","    \"category\": \"deployment\",\n","    \"language\": \"python\",\n","    \"version\": \"3.12\"\n","}\n","```\n","\n","You can use metadata to filter or influence ranking.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","- Filter by language = Python.\n","- Filter by category = deployment.\n","- Prefer documentation for the correct version.\n","- Rerank the remaining documents.\n","\n","The distinction is that metadata filtering may happen before retrieval, while metadata-aware reranking happens after retrieval or as part of a scoring model.\n","\n","### Diversity-aware reranking\n","\n","Imagine the top 5 results are all chunks from the same document. They may be individually relevant, but the LLM receives repetitive information.\n","\n","You can use diversity-aware reranking, such as Maximal Marginal Relevance (MMR), to balance relevance and diversity.\n","\n","The MMR formula is:\n","\n","$$ MMR(D_i)= \\lambda \\cdot Sim(Q,D_i) - (1-\\lambda)\\cdot \\max_{D_j \\in S} Sim(D_i,D_j) $$\n","\n","Where:\n","\n","- $Q$: query\n","- $D_i$: candidate document\n","- $S$: documents already selected\n","- $\\lambda$: relevance-diversity trade-off\n","\n","If $\\lambda$ is close to 1, relevance is prioritized.\n","\n","If $\\lambda$ is closer to 0, diversity is prioritized.\n","\n","MMR is not necessarily a neural cross-encoder reranker. It is a selection and reordering technique.\n","\n","### Query-aware reranking\n","\n","Sometimes the user's query is ambiguous.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","> \"How does memory work?\"\n","\n","This could mean:\n","\n","- RAM in a computer.\n","- Memory management in Python.\n","- Memory in an LLM agent.\n","- Human memory.\n","\n","A system may first rewrite or clarify the query, then retrieve and rerank documents based on the refined query.\n","\n","This is often combined with query expansion or query rewriting.\n","\n","### Multi-stage reranking\n","\n","Advanced retrieval systems can use multiple stages.\n","\n","![Multi Stage Reranking](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/multi_stage_reranking.png)\n","\n","Why use this?\n","\n","- A lightweight reranker is cheaper than a large cross-encoder.\n","- The expensive model only processes a smaller candidate list.\n","- This can improve latency and cost while retaining retrieval quality.\n","\n","## Evaluate Reranking Quality\n","\n","If you're an AI engineer, you need to know how to evaluate whether reranking actually improves your RAG pipeline.\n","\n","You should not simply say:\n","\n","> \"The reranker gives better results.\"\n","\n","You should measure the improvement.\n","\n","### Recall@K\n","\n","Recall@K measures whether relevant documents are retrieved within the top K results.\n","\n","$$ Recall@K = \\frac{\\text{Relevant documents retrieved in top K}} {\\text{Total relevant documents}} $$\n","\n","Suppose there are 10 relevant documents.\n","\n","The retrieval system finds 8 within the top 50.\n","\n","$$ Recall@50=\\frac{8}{10}=0.8 $$\n","\n","Recall@50 = 80%\n","\n","Recall is especially important for the initial retrieval stage.\n","\n","### Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR)\n","\n","MRR measures how highly the first relevant document appears.\n","\n","$$ MRR=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\frac{1}{rank_i} $$\n","\n","Suppose:\n","\n","- Query 1: First relevant document is rank 1.\n","- Query 2: First relevant document is rank 2.\n","- Query 3: First relevant document is rank 4.\n","\n","Then:\n","\n","$$ MRR= \\frac{1}{3} \\left( 1+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\right) $$\n","\n","$$ MRR=0.5833 $$\n","\n","Higher MRR means relevant results tend to appear earlier.\n","\n","### NDCG@K\n","\n","Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain measures ranking quality, including graded relevance.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","- Highly relevant document: relevance 3.\n","- Relevant document: relevance 2.\n","- Somewhat relevant: relevance 1.\n","- Irrelevant document: relevance 0.\n","\n","The formula:\n","\n","$$ DCG@K=\\sum_{i=1}^{K} \\frac{2^{rel_i}-1}{\\log_2(i+1)} $$\n","\n","Then:\n","\n","$$ NDCG@K=\\frac{DCG@K}{IDCG@K} $$\n","\n","Where $IDCG$ is the ideal DCG for the same query.\n","\n","NDCG is particularly useful when relevance is not just binary.\n","\n","### Before-and-after evaluation\n","\n","You can compare:\n","\n","| Metric          | Vector Search | Vector + Reranker |\n","| --------------- | ------------- | ----------------- |\n","| Recall@50      | 0.92          | 0.92              |\n","| MRR@10         | 0.62          | 0.81              |\n","| NDCG@10        | 0.68          | 0.85              |\n","| Average latency | 80 ms         | 240 ms            |\n","\n","Illustrative benchmark, not measured results.\n","\n","Notice:\n","\n","Recall@50 remains the same because the reranker reorders the retrieved candidates but does not change the candidate set.\n","\n","MRR and NDCG improve because relevant documents move toward the top.\n","\n","The latency increases because the reranker adds another model inference step.\n","\n","## Problems in Production\n","\n","| Problem                             | Cause                                  | Solution                                                       |\n","| ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n","| Relevant document missing           | Initial retrieval recall is poor       | Increase candidate K, improve embeddings, use hybrid retrieval |\n","| High latency                        | Too many query-document pairs          | Reduce candidate count, batch inference, use a smaller model   |\n","| Reranker score is misleading        | Scores treated as probabilities        | Calibrate scores or use rank-based evaluation                  |\n","| Duplicate chunks in top results     | Similar chunks from same source        | Deduplicate or use diversity-aware selection                   |\n","| Wrong domain ranking                | Reranker trained on a different domain | Evaluate domain performance, fine-tune if justified            |\n","| Context exceeds LLM limit           | Too many or too-large chunks           | Select fewer chunks, compress or trim context                  |\n","| Relevant information gets truncated | Cross-encoder input length limit       | Chunk documents appropriately and inspect truncation           |\n","| Increased infrastructure cost       | Dedicated model serving                | Batch, optimize, cache where appropriate                       |\n","\n","### Important advanced insight: Chunk size\n","\n","Reranking quality depends significantly on document chunking.\n","\n","Suppose you have a 10,000-token document.\n","\n","You split it into chunks:\n","\n","```\n","Document\n","  ├── Chunk 1\n","  ├── Chunk 2\n","  ├── Chunk 3\n","  ├── Chunk 4\n","  └── Chunk 5\n","```\n","\n","The reranker scores each chunk independently against the query.\n","\n","If the relevant answer is hidden inside a very large chunk, the model may not evaluate it effectively because of its input-length limit.\n","\n","If the chunk is too small, context may be lost.\n","\n","Therefore, chunking and reranking should be tuned together.\n","\n","## Interview Questions\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is reranking in a vector database?</summary>\n","\n","Reranking is a second-stage retrieval process that reorders candidate documents based on their relevance to a user query. It typically uses a cross-encoder model to score query-document pairs.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why do we need reranking if vector search already retrieves relevant documents?</summary>\n","\n","Vector search is optimized for fast candidate retrieval. Embedding similarity may not capture precise query-document relevance. Reranking provides a more detailed relevance assessment and improves the ordering of retrieved candidates.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is the difference between a bi-encoder and a cross-encoder?</summary>\n","\n","A bi-encoder independently encodes the query and document into vectors, then compares them using similarity. A cross-encoder jointly processes the query and document to produce a relevance score.\n","\n","Bi-encoders are fast and suitable for large-scale retrieval. Cross-encoders are generally more accurate for fine-grained ranking but computationally more expensive.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Can reranking retrieve documents that vector search missed?</summary>\n","\n","No. A standard reranker only reorders the documents it receives. If the relevant document is not in the candidate set, the reranker cannot select it.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is the difference between cosine similarity and reranker score?</summary>\n","\n","Cosine similarity measures the similarity between embedding vectors. A reranker score is produced by a relevance model evaluating the query and document together. The scores have different meanings and scales.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is the difference between RRF and cross-encoder reranking?</summary>\n","\n","RRF combines rankings from multiple retrieval systems, such as BM25 and vector search, using their rank positions.\n","\n","Cross-encoder reranking evaluates the relevance of each query-document pair using a neural model.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why do we retrieve 50 or 100 documents and then select 5?</summary>\n","\n","The larger candidate pool improves the chance of retrieving relevant information. The reranker then selects the most relevant documents to reduce the context size and improve efficiency.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>How would you improve a poor reranking system?</summary>\n","\n","I would:\n","\n","1. Evaluate candidate retrieval recall.\n","2. Improve the embedding model if retrieval recall is low.\n","3. Increase the candidate pool if latency permits.\n","4. Evaluate the reranker against labeled query-document pairs.\n","5. Tune chunk size and overlap.\n","6. Consider hybrid retrieval.\n","7. Measure NDCG@K and MRR@K.\n","8. Optimize inference latency and batching.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Can you fine-tune a reranker?</summary>\n","\n","Yes. A reranker can be fine-tuned using query-document relevance data.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```\n","Query:\n","\"What is FAISS?\"\n","\n","Positive document:\n","\"FAISS is a library for efficient similarity search.\"\n","\n","Negative document:\n","\"FastAPI is a Python web framework.\"\n","```\n","\n","The training objective encourages the model to score positive documents higher than negative documents.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Is reranking always necessary in RAG?</summary>\n","\n","No.\n","\n","Reranking adds latency and computational cost. For small datasets, simple semantic search may be sufficient. Reranking becomes more useful when the retrieval system returns many candidates and precise relevance ordering significantly impacts answer quality.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":34,"id":"9d7b3d82","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.507529Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.507034Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.512425Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.511565Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.019718,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.514419+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.494701+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["retriever = vectorstore.as_retriever(\n","    search_type=\"similarity\",\n","    search_kwargs={\"k\": 3}\n",")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"2da78e10","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.010791,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.536122+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.525331+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# Evaluation\n","\n","A chatbot that produces fluent answers is not necessarily a good RAG system. You need an evaluation dataset containing representative questions, expected answers, and the documents or passages that should support them.\n","\n","## Build a test dataset"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":35,"id":"e7a3f7a5","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.559838Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.559401Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.56491Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.563923Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.019649,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.566974+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.547325+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["evaluation_data = [    \n","    {        \n","        \"question\": \"How long is the refund window?\",\n","        \"expected_answer\": \"30 days\",\n","        \"relevant_chunk_ids\": [\n","            \"refund-policy-v1:chunk-0\"\n","        ],\n","    },{\n","        \"question\": \"How long does an approved refund take?\",\n","        \"expected_answer\": \"5 business days\",\n","        \"relevant_chunk_ids\": [\n","        \"refund-policy-v1:chunk-1\"\n","        ],\n","    }\n","]"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":36,"id":"38171738","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.591057Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.590125Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.596216Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.595342Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.020062,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.598+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.577938+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["\n","# Ground-truth relevance judgments\n","# 0 = irrelevant, 1 = relevant, 2 = highly relevant\n","\n","ground_truth = {\n","    \"q1\": {\n","        \"doc1\": 2,\n","        \"doc2\": 1,\n","        \"doc3\": 0\n","    },\n","    \"q2\": {\n","        \"doc4\": 2,\n","        \"doc5\": 1\n","    },\n","    \"q3\": {\n","        \"doc6\": 2,\n","        \"doc7\": 2\n","    }\n","}\n","\n","# Test queries\n","queries = {\n","    \"q1\": \"What is machine learning?\",\n","    \"q2\": \"Explain neural networks\",\n","    \"q3\": \"What is deep learning?\"\n","}"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"184ac2cb","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.010922,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.619766+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.608844+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Include a variety of cases:\n","\n","- Simple factual questions.\n","- Questions requiring multiple passages.\n","- Paraphrased questions.\n","- Exact identifiers and rare terms.\n","- Questions with no answer in the corpus.\n","- Ambiguous questions.\n","- Conflicting or outdated sources.\n","- Permission-sensitive questions.\n","- Long questions and conversational follow-ups.\n","\n","## Retrieval metrics\n","\n","| Metric       | Meaning                                                   |\n","| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n","| Precision\\@K | Fraction of the top K retrieved chunks that are relevant  |\n","| Recall\\@K    | Fraction of all relevant chunks found in the top K        |\n","| MRR          | Rewards retrieving the first relevant result near the top |\n","| nDCG\\@K      | Evaluates ranking quality, including graded relevance     |\n","| Hit Rate\\@K  | Whether at least one relevant chunk appears in the top K  |\n","\n","For a simple case, suppose there are four retrieved chunks and two are relevant.\n","\n","$$\n","\\text{Precision@4} = \\frac{2}{4}=0.5\n","$$\n","\n","If there are four relevant chunks in the entire corpus and the retriever finds two:\n","\n","$$\n","\\text{Recall@4}=\\frac{2}{4}=0.5\n","$$\n","\n","High precision with low recall means the results are mostly relevant but may omit necessary evidence. High recall with low precision means the retriever finds the evidence but also returns much irrelevant material.\n","\n","For multi-passage questions, evaluate whether the retriever finds all necessary evidence, not merely one relevant chunk."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":37,"id":"d26646a1","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.643023Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.642633Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-23T06:10:56.611379Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-23T06:10:56.609956Z"},"papermill":{"duration":5.982896,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:56.613424+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:50.630528+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"]}],"source":["%pip install -q ranx"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":38,"id":"05a4c25e","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-23T06:10:56.638155Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-23T06:10:56.637013Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-23T06:11:18.241061Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-23T06:11:18.239964Z"},"papermill":{"duration":21.619029,"end_time":"2026-11-23T06:11:18.243375+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-23T06:10:56.624346+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["from ranx import Qrels\n","\n","# Convert ground truth into Ranx Qrels\n","qrels = Qrels(ground_truth)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":39,"id":"afedffd4","metadata":{"exe